      "topic_id": "010921c9-91f2-475a-aa4f-6cb9babba0e5",
      "text": "In architectural project management, what is the safest source-based meaning of scope?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "A vague design ambition that can change without written effect"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "The agreed service and deliverable boundary that controls what the architect must produce"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "Only the contractor schedule"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "Only the owner budget ceiling"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "b",
      "correct_value": null,
      "accepted_answers": null,
      "value_tolerance": 0,
      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "Scope is the agreed boundary of services and deliverables, not an informal wish list.",
      "explanation": "Confuses scope with building size; scope is about deliverables and services, not building dimensions. Confuses scope with budget; scope defines what services are included, not how much the construction costs. Confuses scope with location; scope defines deliverables and services, not where the building is built. Scope is the defined set of deliverables and services—project phases, deliverables per phase, included and excluded services, and client responsibilities. Scope is what the architect will deliver and provide, not the building size, cost, or location.",
